= Inference =

== Overview ==
'''Inference''' is the process or relation by which a conclusion is derived, supported or reached from premises, [[Evidence|evidence]], rules, models or representations.

Inference is central to logic, science, statistics, artificial intelligence and everyday reasoning.

Different kinds of inference provide different strengths of support.

== Premises and conclusion ==
A simple inference has:

<pre>
premise 1
premise 2
...
therefore conclusion
</pre>

Example:

<pre>
All mammals are animals.
Whales are mammals.
Therefore whales are animals.
</pre>

This is a deductive inference.

== Deduction ==
'''Deductive inference''' aims to preserve truth.

If the premises are true and the inference is valid, the conclusion must be true.

Validity concerns structure.

A valid argument can still have a false conclusion if one or more premises are false.

Therefore:

<pre>
valid inference ≠ guaranteed true premises
</pre>

== Induction ==
'''Inductive inference''' generalizes from observed cases or data.

Example:

<pre>
many observed samples have property P
→ unobserved cases probably have P
</pre>

Induction is ampliative: the conclusion goes beyond what is logically guaranteed by the premises.

Statistical estimation and prediction often involve induction.

== Abduction ==
'''Abductive inference''' proposes or selects an explanation for observed evidence.

Example:

<pre>
If H were true, E would be expected.
E occurred.
H is a plausible explanation.
</pre>

This is not deductively valid by itself.

Alternative explanations must be compared.

== Entailment ==
'''Entailment''' is a semantic relation.

A set of statements Γ entails φ when every relevant interpretation/model satisfying Γ also satisfies φ:

<pre>
Γ ⊨ φ
</pre>

Entailment is not an algorithm.

It states what logically follows under the semantics.

== Inference procedure ==
An inference procedure is a method/algorithm for deriving conclusions.

Examples:
* resolution;
* forward chaining;
* backward chaining;
* tableau reasoning;
* theorem proving;
* probabilistic inference.

The relationship between a procedure and semantics is evaluated using soundness and completeness.

== Soundness ==
An inference procedure is '''sound''' when it derives only conclusions that are semantically entailed.

Informally:

<pre>
derivable → entailed
</pre>

A sound system does not invent invalid logical consequences within its formal semantics.

== Completeness ==
An inference procedure is '''complete''' for a logic/problem when every semantically entailed conclusion in scope can be derived.

Informally:

<pre>
entailed → derivable
</pre>

Soundness and completeness are different properties.

A procedure can be sound but incomplete.

== Monotonic inference ==
An inference system is monotonic when adding premises cannot invalidate an existing consequence.

Classical logic and standard OWL reasoning are generally monotonic.

Formally:

<pre>
Γ ⊨ φ
→
Γ ∪ Δ ⊨ φ
</pre>

== Non-monotonic inference ==
Human commonsense reasoning often uses defaults that can be defeated by new information.

Example:

<pre>
birds normally fly
Tweety is a bird
→ Tweety flies

new information:
Tweety is a penguin
</pre>

The earlier conclusion can be withdrawn.

Default logic and other non-monotonic systems model such behavior.

== Probabilistic inference ==
Probabilistic inference updates or computes probability distributions.

For Bayesian inference:

<pre>
P(H | E) ∝ P(E | H)P(H)
</pre>

The result depends on the model, likelihood, prior and evidence.

Probabilistic support is different from deductive entailment.

== Causal inference ==
[[Causality|Causal inference]] estimates causal relations/effects using interventions, assumptions and statistical models.

A strong association alone does not establish causation.

Causal inference therefore combines evidence with structural assumptions beyond ordinary correlation.

== RDF inference ==
[[Resource Description Framework|RDF]] semantics defines entailment regimes.

For example, RDFS can infer superclass membership.

An RDF processor is not required to materialize every valid inference unless its application contract says so.

Validity of an inference and obligation to compute it are distinct.

== OWL inference ==
[[Web Ontology Language|OWL]] reasoners can infer:
* subclass relations;
* class membership;
* equivalence;
* inconsistency;
* property consequences.

These conclusions follow from ontology axioms under OWL semantics.

They are not statistical predictions.

== Knowledge graph inference ==
[[Knowledge graph|Knowledge graphs]] can use several inference styles:

* symbolic entailment;
* rules;
* graph algorithms;
* probabilistic reasoning;
* embedding-based link prediction.

A system should distinguish:

<pre>
explicit assertion
≠ logically inferred assertion
≠ statistically predicted edge
</pre>

The provenance and epistemic status differ.

== Inference and knowledge ==
A correct inference can transmit justification only when the premises and reasoning conditions are epistemically appropriate.

Formal validity alone cannot convert false or unsupported premises into [[Knowledge|knowledge]].

This links inference with [[Evidence]], [[Justification]] and [[Epistemology]].

== Epistemic notes ==
"Inferred" should always invite the question: under which premises, semantics, model and procedure? Different inference systems can produce different legitimate conclusions from differently structured assumptions.

